 `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The planner has to evaluate `lower()` on every row. This is the main problem, and it grows with the size of `users`.\n2. **Scan of `orders` on `user_id`.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, each matching user forces either a full scan of `orders` or a hash join that reads all of it. Foreign keys aren't indexed automatically in Postgres or MySQL/InnoDB (InnoDB does create one), so check your schema.\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any large text/JSON columns. It also rules out index-only scans. Both tables have an `id` column and probably others that collide, which makes the result set ambiguous for clients that map columns by name.\n\n## Proposed fixes\n\n**1. Index that matches the predicate** (Postgres syntax):\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n```\nIf emails are unique case-insensitively, make it `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX`. That also gives the planner a one-row guarantee. In MySQL 8, use a functional index `((lower(email)))`, or rely on a case-insensitive collation and drop `lower()`. In SQL Server, use a computed column plus an index, or a case-insensitive collation.\n\n**2. Index the join key:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\nIf you usually want recent orders first, use `(user_id, created_at DESC)` instead. That avoids a sort and supports `LIMIT`.\n\n**3. Rewrite to name columns:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\nAdjust the column list to what the caller needs. Also normalize the input with `lower()` on the literal side, or in the application, so the comparison can't silently miss. The index on `lower(email)` still applies.\n\n## Expected plan after the changes\nAn index scan on `users_email_lower_idx` returns one row. A nested-loop index scan on `orders_user_id_idx` follows. The work then scales with that user's order count instead of table size.\n\n## Caveats\n- I only had the query. I haven't seen the schema, row counts, or existing indexes, so confirm with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after.\n- If the user could have thousands of orders, add pagination (`ORDER BY ... LIMIT`) to the query.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}